# Problem 05: ICA of Audio Signals

## Audio reading

> Two artificially mixed audio files were imported. Their sampling rates were recorded, and only the left audio channels were retained for analysis.

## Equal length করা

> Since ICA requires observations of equal length, both audio signals were truncated to the length of the shorter signal and combined into a two-column matrix.

## FastICA

```r
separated <- fastICA(signal_comb, n.comp = 2)
```

> FastICA extracted two statistically independent components from the two observed audio mixtures. The resulting components represented estimates of the original source sounds.

## Normalization

```r
separated_1 <- separated$S[,1] /
               max(abs(separated$S[,1]))
```

এখানে আপনার code একটু সংশোধন করা ভালো। Original code-এ শুধু `max()` আছে। Negative amplitude বড় হলে সমস্যা হতে পারে। তাই `max(abs())` বেশি সঠিক।

Interpretation:

> The separated signals were normalized so that their amplitudes remained within a suitable range for playback and storage.

## Final conclusion

> After listening to the separated audio files, the two underlying sounds could be distinguished more clearly than in the original mixed recordings. Thus, ICA demonstrated its ability to solve the blind source separation problem.

