# Problem 06: Psychological–Academic CCA

এখানে:

* `X` = তিনটি psychological variables
* `Y` = চারটি academic variables

সুতরাং সর্বোচ্চ canonical functions:

$$
\min(3,4)=3
$$

## Correlation matrices

> The within-set correlation matrices showed how strongly variables within the psychological and academic groups were related. The between-set correlation matrix provided preliminary evidence regarding the relationships between psychological characteristics and academic performance.

## Canonical correlations

> Three canonical correlations were obtained because the smaller psychological set contained three variables. The first canonical correlation was the largest, meaning that the first pair of canonical variates represented the strongest relationship between psychological characteristics and academic performance.

## Four significance tests

Code-এ আছে:

* Wilks
* Hotelling
* Pillai
* Roy

Interpretation:

> Wilks’ Lambda, Hotelling’s Trace, Pillai’s Trace and Roy’s Largest Root were used to test the statistical significance of the canonical functions. A p-value below 0.05 indicated that the corresponding canonical relationship was statistically significant.

## Canonical coefficients

> The raw canonical coefficients represented the weights used to construct the psychological and academic canonical variates. Larger absolute coefficients indicated greater contribution to the construction of the corresponding canonical variate.

## Canonical loadings

```r
loadings.X <- cor(X, xscores)
loadings.Y <- cor(Y, yscores)
```

> Canonical loadings measured the correlations between the original variables and their own canonical variates. Variables with high absolute loadings were considered the most important variables for interpreting each canonical function.

## Cross-loadings

> Cross-loadings measured the correlations between variables in one set and the canonical variates of the opposite set. They showed how strongly individual psychological variables were related to the academic canonical variates and vice versa.

## Scatter plots

> The first canonical-pair plot showed the strongest relationship between the psychological and academic canonical scores. A clear linear pattern around the regression line indicated a strong canonical relationship. The second pair was expected to show a weaker relationship because canonical correlations decrease successively.

## Final Problem 06 conclusion

> Overall, CCA revealed whether a significant multivariate relationship existed between psychological characteristics and academic performance. The first canonical function generally provided the most meaningful relationship, while subsequent functions explained progressively smaller remaining associations.

